Conductor CLI updater and task-relevant lessons — acceptance ledger, 2026-09-27

Scope: local installed components, official-source acquisition, ownership, language,
private memory/settings preservation, recoverable update and CLI delivery/removal.
Companion upgrades, project-adapter updates and automatic Cursor UI changes excluded.
This is an implementation acceptance record, not a new whole-project audit.

Observed initial checks:
- Windows updater suite: 32 tests, exit 0, one POSIX symlink test skipped.
- Linux updater suite: 32 tests, exit 0, all executed.
- Windows task recall: 16 tests, exit 0, two symlink privilege cases skipped.
- Windows memory delivery/maintenance: 10 tests, exit 0.
- Windows evidence: 80 tests, exit 0, three platform-specific cases skipped.
- Windows settings JSON: 20 tests, exit 0.

Refutations during implementation:
- Concurrent edit after prepare: assertion FAIL without guarded preflight observations;
  guard now binds the transaction to the earlier bytes. Fresh targeted test exit 0.
- Linux installer mode mismatch: identical content hashes, actual mode 0755 vs recorded
  0644. Registration now records observed installed modes. Fresh installer/update test exit 0.
- Snapshot recovery size: 13 MiB source produced a backup larger than the reader's limit.
  Test failed before fix; oversized backup now refuses before target writes, test exit 0.
- Existing memory tests isolated only CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, not HOME. New launcher delivery
  exposed the incomplete sandbox. Both memory installer fixtures now isolate HOME and
  USERPROFILE. Two newly created test launchers in the real profile were identified by
  exact snapshot hashes and prior absence, then moved into their backup for recovery.
  No pre-existing command was overwritten; no private memory was changed by those tests.

Final affected-suite checks:
- python -B -m unittest discover -s qa/updater -v: Windows 37 tests, exit 0,
  two POSIX cases skipped; Linux 37 tests, exit 0, one Windows case skipped.
- Windows installed-hook verification also passed from PowerShell, where PATH bash
  resolves to WSL; explicit Git Bash selection prevents the observed hook-smoke failure.
- Linux mode-only rollback now restores permissions and refuses later user chmod.
  The original test failed (0644 != 0755). Content-only equality caused the skip;
  the alternative hypothesis (filesystem ignored chmod) was excluded by stat readings.
- Existing memory 10, recall 16, evidence 80, memory restore 14 tests passed on both OSes;
  platform-only cases skipped explicitly. Windows settings JSON 20 tests passed.
- Doctor integration, uninstall 7 scenarios, lint both locales, lint negative self-test
  32/32 and shell syntax passed. These unchanged checks are reused after updater-only fixes.
- Isolated CURRENT copies retained CURRENT tests: preflight edit and backup-size guards,
  rollback permissions and Windows Bash selection each PASS -> assertion FAIL -> PASS.

Live deployment: both installers completed in Russian with companion installation skipped.
213 private file hashes (lessons, curated/project memory, private Git metadata, backup
script and language) were identical before/after; foreign settings preserved. Runtime
and global rules were snapshotted outside the public Git set. Installed conductor status
reports claude/global/values, Russian. Bootstrap revision is null, not a claimed release.

Published implementation: main 5a507120731fe70c5dd7e83b5df6f8cdc6b81f10.
The final bootstrap delivered the two acceptance fixes, then the installed Windows CLI
ran from the normal Windows environment, outside the repository, against official GitHub:
- conductor update --check: exit 0, one state file planned; installed bytes unchanged.
- conductor update: exit 0, verification passed, recoverable snapshot printed.
- conductor update again: exit 0, zero changes, no-op message and byte comparison.
- conductor status: Russian, claude/global/values, exact published revision above.
All 213 private hashes and settings.json bytes were unchanged across this acceptance.
Private local logs/snapshots are intentionally git-ignored, never included in publication.

Graph refresh is separate: the first CLI extraction returned exit 0 despite missing
CHANGELOG output and shrinking Azerbaijani README coverage. The independent coverage
check rejected it; the public graph was not replaced until the focused retry succeeded.
The incremental merger then lost source ownership on colliding IDs. Reassembling the
complete current semantic cache and fresh AST with Graphify's own build/cluster/export
APIs passed independent acceptance: 49/49 documents, 1436 nodes, 2795 edges, 59 hyperedges,
no duplicate IDs, dangling endpoints, pending inputs or stale document cache. Changed
documents have new generated IDs; unchanged cached concepts remain represented. Reduced
per-source counts include cross-file source reassignment and removed recent-preview code.
No handwritten semantic nodes, partial-save override, new HTML or report was used.

CI follow-up: run 36278806599 passed Linux but failed Windows registration: the shell
embedded RUNNER~1 while Python resolved runneradmin. A case-alias reproduction failed
with the same core.md mismatch; LF/CRLF was excluded by unchanged-byte rendering.
Both installers now canonicalize the configuration path before rendering any rules or
hook registrations. No ownership comparison was weakened. Current memory 10, restore
14 and updater 39 tests passed on Windows/Linux. Windows skipped POSIX cases and the
new 8.3 case because this local volume does not assign distinct short names; the case
alias executed. Linux skipped three Windows cases. Isolated normalization control:
current test PASS -> assertion FAIL with normalization disabled -> PASS restored.

CI follow-up 2: run 36279694993 passed Linux, then failed Windows recall assertions.
The returned source was the correct file/content, but tests compared abspath with
resolve (RUNNER~1 vs runneradmin). A local case-alias test reproduced assertion FAIL;
samefile and byte equality excluded wrong-source selection. Expectations now preserve
absolute caller spelling, matching recall's deliberate refusal to resolve links before
checking them. The same reproduction passes; runtime recall was not weakened or changed.
Final recall suite: 17 tests, Windows exit 0 (two privileged symlink cases skipped),
Linux exit 0 (one Windows case skipped); Linux link/FIFO protections executed.

The real installer flow also now runs doctor. It exposed doctor's original-path-only
comparison after canonicalized installation. Doctor tries either exact path spelling,
without relaxing hook structure. Current isolated case-alias test: PASS -> assertion
FAIL with only the canonical fallback disabled -> PASS restored, source hashes matched.
Doctor negative integration checks passed on both OSes. Final updater suite with doctor:
39 tests, Windows and Linux exit 0, three documented platform skips on each.
The final CI result belongs to the new publication; earlier failed runs are not green.
